From: Tomonori Nakagawa <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: sprd: wait for audcp rather than dropping the write

The digital half of the codec lives in the audcp power domain and only
answers while the audio DSP is out of deep sleep, so sprd_codec_write()
checks agdsp_can_access() before touching it. When that check fails it
discards the write and returns 0 - success - so the caller carries on
believing it has programmed a route it has not.

agdsp_access_enable(), called immediately above, is not enough of a
guarantee. It polls for the power domain to come up, but only when it is
taking the first reference (ap_enable_cnt == 0); a later caller just
increments the count and returns. And the thing it polls for -
audcp_pmu_pwr_status4 reaching PD_WAKEUP_STATE - is the domain being
powered, which is not the same condition agdsp_can_access() tests: that
one also wants the deep-sleep bit clear in audcp_pmu_sleep_ctrl and the
sleep status clear. A write can therefore land in the window where the
domain is up but the core has not finished waking.

The result is a bring-up that works or does not depending on timing. On
RG Rotate the speaker path came up perhaps half the time, and reliably
came up when anything was run alongside it that added latency - starting
"dmesg -w" in another shell was enough to make it work every time.

Give the write the same bounded wait the wake path uses instead, and
report -EIO if the core never becomes reachable, so a genuinely
unreachable DSP is a visible failure rather than a silent one. 20 ms is
generous next to the wake poll's own 5 s ceiling and short enough not to
matter if it is ever hit.

The read side already does the right thing here: it returns the shadowed
value rather than a false zero, because its caller is usually
snd_soc_component_update_bits() and a zero would corrupt the register.

Signed-off-by: Tomonori Nakagawa <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>
---
diff --git a/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-codec.c b/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-codec.c
index d32459d9a1e4..d9bef0f7b4b8 100755
--- a/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-codec.c
+++ b/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-codec.c
@@ -199,6 +199,26 @@ static void sprd_codec_psg_state_exit(struct snd_soc_component *codec);
 
 static unsigned long sprd_codec_dp_base;
 
+/*
+ * Wait for the audio DSP to be reachable. agdsp_access_enable() has already
+ * asked for it; this only covers the window between the power domain coming
+ * up and the core leaving deep sleep. Returns true once the DP register
+ * window can be touched.
+ */
+#define SPRD_CODEC_AGDSP_WAIT_US	20000
+static bool sprd_codec_wait_agdsp_access(void)
+{
+	unsigned int waited;
+
+	for (waited = 0; waited < SPRD_CODEC_AGDSP_WAIT_US; waited += 50) {
+		if (agdsp_can_access())
+			return true;
+		udelay(50);
+	}
+
+	return agdsp_can_access();
+}
+
 /*
  * Write-through shadow of the digital (DP) register window.
  *
@@ -3414,12 +3434,24 @@ static int sprd_codec_write(struct snd_soc_component *codec, unsigned int reg,
 			pr_err("%s, agdsp_access_enable failed!\n", __func__);
 			return ret;
 		}
-		/* see sprd_codec_read(): don't touch DP regs if audcp is asleep */
-		if (!agdsp_can_access()) {
-			pr_warn_ratelimited("%s: audcp not accessible, DP reg 0x%x write dropped\n",
-					    __func__, reg);
+		/*
+		 * see sprd_codec_read(): the DP window only answers while
+		 * audcp is out of deep sleep. agdsp_access_enable() polls the
+		 * power domain, but only when it is the first reference, and
+		 * the domain being powered is not the same thing as the core
+		 * being awake - so a write can still arrive here while the
+		 * core is asleep. Give it the same bounded wait rather than
+		 * dropping the write: a dropped register write used to be
+		 * reported as success, which left the caller believing it had
+		 * programmed a route it had not, and turned speaker bring-up
+		 * into a coin flip that anything adding latency (even running
+		 * dmesg -w alongside it) would "fix".
+		 */
+		if (!sprd_codec_wait_agdsp_access()) {
+			pr_err_ratelimited("%s: audcp still asleep, DP reg 0x%x not written\n",
+					   __func__, reg);
 			agdsp_access_disable();
-			return 0;
+			return -EIO;
 		}
 		codec_digital_reg_enable(codec);
 		sp_asoc_pr_reg("D[0x%04x] R:[0x%08x]\n",
